{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"The magnitude of alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) power suppression in occipital sensors following phoneme onset correlates negatively with phoneme duration, indicating stronger attentional engagement for shorter phonemes.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether greater post‐onset alpha suppression in occipital channels is associated with shorter phoneme durations.",
      "steps":"1. Identify occipital gradiometer channels by parsing channel_names for codes in the 171–199 range (back‐of‐head sensors). 2. For each session, load MEG data and phoneme events (kind=='phoneme'). 3. Epoch data from −0.5 to 0.5 s around each phoneme timemeg. 4. Bandpass filter epochs in 8–12 Hz. 5. For each epoch and channel, compute mean alpha power in −0.5–0 s (baseline) and 0–0.5 s (post‐onset). 6. Compute suppression as percent decrease: (baseline–post)/baseline×100, then average across occipital channels. 7. For all epochs, correlate suppression values with phoneme duration using Pearson’s r. 8. Test significance with a permutation test (shuffling durations 1,000 times).",
      "deliverables":"1. List of occipital channels used. 2. Per‐epoch suppression vs. duration scatterplot and Pearson r with p‐value. 3. Permutation‐test significance."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Functional connectivity (coherence) between left auditory and left inferior frontal ROIs increases transiently after word onset compared to matched baseline periods.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Measure band‐limited coherence change between auditory and frontal regions at word onsets.",
      "steps":"1. Define left auditory ROI (channels codes 11–49) and left inferior frontal ROI (codes 51–79) from channel_names. 2. For each session, load MEG data and word events (kind=='word'). 3. Extract 0.5–1.0 s baseline windows and 0–0.5 s post‐onset epochs around timemeg. 4. Compute cross‐spectral density and coherence between all pairs of channels (one from auditory ROI, one from frontal ROI) in the beta band (15–25 Hz) using multitaper or Welch method. 5. Average coherence across all channel pairs and trials for each condition. 6. For each session, perform a paired t‐test comparing baseline vs. post‐onset coherence values. 7. Across sessions, perform a one‐sample t‐test on session‐wise coherence increases.",
      "deliverables":"1. ROI channel lists. 2. Session‐wise coherence means and t‐statistics. 3. Grand mean coherence increase and group‐level p‐value."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Phase‐amplitude coupling (PAC) between theta phase (4–8 Hz) and gamma amplitude (30–50 Hz) in bilateral auditory ROIs is stronger for phonemes later in the word (higher phonemeidx) than for initial phonemes.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether theta–gamma coupling increases with phoneme position within words.",
      "steps":"1. Identify bilateral auditory ROIs from channel_codes (11–49 left; 231–269 right). 2. For each session, load MEG and phoneme events. 3. Group phoneme epochs into early (phonemeidx==1) vs. late (phonemeidx>=max−1) within each word. 4. For each group, epoch −0.2 to 0.3 s around timemeg. 5. Bandpass filter epochs at 4–8 Hz for phase and 30–50 Hz for amplitude. 6. Compute PAC (e.g., Modulation Index) per epoch per ROI using established toolbox. 7. Compare PAC distributions between early vs. late phonemes with paired t‐tests per session and one‐sample t‐test across sessions.",
      "deliverables":"1. Early vs. late PAC values per session. 2. Session‐wise and group‐level statistics. 3. Example comodulograms."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The latency of the M100 auditory evoked field following word onset is inversely correlated with word duration, with shorter words eliciting earlier peaks.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Determine whether earlier M100 peaks are associated with shorter spoken words.",
      "steps":"1. Select temporal gradiometers around auditory cortex (codes 21–29 left and 241–249 right). 2. For each session, load MEG and word events. 3. Epoch data −0.1 to 0.3 s around each word timemeg. 4. Baseline‐correct each epoch using −0.1–0 s. 5. Average epochs across the two temporal ROIs separately to get two ERFs per word. 6. In each ERF, detect the largest deflection between 0.05–0.15 s as the M100 peak; record its latency. 7. Correlate peak latency with reported word duration using Spearman’s rho. 8. Assess significance with permutation of durations (1,000 shuffles).",
      "deliverables":"1. M100 latency per word. 2. Scatterplot and Spearman rho with p‐value. 3. Permutation test results."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The 1/f spectral slope of resting‐state MEG power in silence segments systematically changes across sessions, reflecting fatigue or adaptation over the experiment.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Estimate and compare the power‐law exponent of MEG PSD in silence periods across sessions.",
      "steps":"1. Identify all silence events (kind=='silence') and extract long continuous segments (duration>=2 s). 2. For each session, concatenate those MEG data windows across all channels. 3. Compute PSD per channel using Welch’s method (1‐100 Hz). 4. Fit the log–log PSD to a linear model in the 10–40 Hz range to estimate the 1/f exponent per channel. 5. Average the exponent across channels to get one slope per session. 6. Test for a linear trend of slope vs. session number using linear regression. 7. Evaluate significance of trend (p‐value of slope coefficient).",
      "deliverables":"1. Per‐session spectral slopes. 2. Scatterplot of slope vs. session with trend line. 3. Regression statistics."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The magnitude of beta‐band (15–25 Hz) rebound following phoneme onset is greater when the phoneme occurs in early sentences of a chapter compared to late sentences.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test sentence position effects on post‐onset beta rebound.",
      "steps":"1. Load phoneme events and extract timechapter and timesentence for grouping. 2. Define early‐sentence phonemes (timesentence <= median) vs. late‐sentence. 3. For each session, epoch −0.2 to 0.8 s around timemeg for each group. 4. Bandpass filter epochs in 15–25 Hz; compute analytic amplitude via Hilbert transform. 5. For each epoch, compute baseline‐normalized amplitude time course; measure rebound as mean amplitude in 0.3–0.6 s post‐onset minus mean in −0.2–0 s. 6. Compare rebound between early vs. late sentences with paired t‐tests per session and group‐level one‐sample t‐test on differences.",
      "deliverables":"1. Rebound magnitudes per group. 2. Session‐wise t‐tests and group‐level result. 3. Time‐course plots of beta amplitude."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"The spatial topography of evoked fields at 100 ms post‐onset differs systematically between word and phoneme events, reflecting hierarchical processing stages.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare MEG topographies at the M100 latency for words vs. phonemes.",
      "steps":"1. For each session, load MEG and both word and phoneme events. 2. Epoch −0.1 to 0.2 s around each event type. 3. Baseline‐correct epochs. 4. Compute grand‐average ERF for words and phonemes separately across all trials. 5. Identify the 100 ms timepoint in each average. 6. Extract sensor‐space topographies at that latency. 7. Compute spatial correlation (e.g., Pearson) between word vs. phoneme topographies. 8. Assess significance via permutation: shuffle labels of single‐trial maps and recompute grand‐averages 1,000 times to build null distribution of correlation.",
      "deliverables":"1. Word and phoneme topography maps at 100 ms. 2. Observed spatial correlation and null distribution with p‐value. 3. Visualization of topography differences."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Baseline alpha‐band (8–12 Hz) power prior to silence onsets predicts the duration of subsequent silence segments, with higher alpha indicating longer pauses.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Determine whether pre‐silence alpha power modulates the duration of upcoming silence.",
      "steps":"1. Load MEG and silence events (kind=='silence') for each session. 2. For each silence segment with duration>=0.2 s, epoch −0.5 to 0 s before its timemeg. 3. Bandpass filter epochs in 8–12 Hz and compute mean power across all channels and timepoints. 4. For each epoch, record baseline alpha power and subsequent silence duration. 5. Across all epochs in a session, compute Spearman correlation between baseline power and silence duration. 6. Test significance with permutation (shuffling durations). 7. Aggregate correlation coefficients across sessions and test with a one‐sample t‐test.",
      "deliverables":"1. Session‐wise correlations and p‐values. 2. Group‐level mean correlation and p‐value. 3. Scatterplots of alpha power vs. silence duration."
    }
  }
]}
